<Project>
    <!-- MSBuild applies only the CLOSEST Directory.Build.props by default.
         To inherit the repo-root settings (Nullable, LangVersion, AutoVersioning,
         debug-type policy, etc.) on top of the src-only additions below, the
         root file must be explicitly imported.  GetPathOfFileAbove walks up
         the directory tree to find the next Directory.Build.props above this
         one — at the repo root, in this case.  Same idiom as tests/. -->
    <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!--
          Make every shipped assembly trim-ANALYSED, not merely trim-ABLE.

          Why this exists — measured 2026-09-03, and it is not what it looks like:

          ILLink only reports trim warnings for assemblies marked IsTrimmable.
          Everything else it trims SILENTLY.  Every project under src/ was
          unmarked, so both apps' Release publishes were trimming the whole
          shared stack while reporting nothing about it.  A real IL2026 in
          AgentForge.Sdk/McpServers/McpServersAccessor.cs sat in shared code
          reachable from BOTH apps and neither publish said a word.

          The reason that bug was ever seen is a command-line accident:
          `dotnet publish -p:PublishTrimmed=true` sets a GLOBAL property, which
          flows into every project in the build graph and switches on each one's
          Roslyn trim analyser.  The same property written inside an app's own
          csproj applies to that app alone, so it never reaches the libraries.
          Enabling PublishTrimmed in OpenCodeForge.csproj therefore produces a
          trim check that trims but stays blind to shared code — which is the
          failure this property closes, and the reason a csproj-only change was
          not sufficient.

          Verified by canary rather than assumed: with the McpServersAccessor
          cast removed, a publish reddens with IL2026 when this property is set
          and stays GREEN (exit 0, zero diagnostics) when it is removed, for
          ClaudeForge as well as OpenCodeForge.  If a future change makes trim
          breaks stop being caught, re-run that canary before trusting the log.

          Cost, measured rather than estimated: zero.  Turning this on surfaced
          no new diagnostics anywhere in src/ at either the Roslyn-analyser or
          the ILLink level, and TrimMode=link was already trimming these
          assemblies — this changes what gets REPORTED, not what gets removed.

          Scoped to src/ deliberately: tests/ has its own Directory.Build.props
          and is never published or trimmed, so analysing it would be noise.
        -->
        <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
